From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:42:17 +0900
Subject: Reduce match aliases in an inline call's arguments before exposing
 opaque types

When an inline method defined beside an opaque type is called, the inliner
looks through each value argument's type for references to the object that
owns the opaque type, and casts the argument to the same type seen through a
proxy that exposes the alias, so that the inlined body can use the argument as
its underlying type. It looked at the widened, dealiased type, which leaves an
applied match alias as it is: for

    type Cell[value] = value match
      case Int => Cell.Whole
      case _   => Cell.Ref[value]

an argument of type `Cell[String]` mentions neither `Cell.Ref` nor the object
`Cell`, so it was not cast, and the body's `ref.get()` was typed against the
still-opaque `Cell.Ref[String]`: "expression does not take parameters".

Typer hides this, because it simplifies the type of every tree it types, the
declared type of a definition included, and simplifying reduces match aliases.
A definition read from TASTy keeps its type as written. So the call failed
whenever the argument was a member of a separately compiled class declared
with such a type, and for every such call in a unit recompiled with
`-from-tasty`.

The argument's type now has its reducible match aliases reduced, at any depth,
before the opaque references are sought in it.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code); reviewed content.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: matchopaque
